## Layout

Two fixed entrypoints, two "add a resource here" folders, one folder for
shared infrastructure — the rest is unopinionated (borrowed from how
Next.js only enforces structure for routing, nothing else):

- `src/{{ package_name }}/main.py` — **the** Flask entrypoint: the
  `create_app()` factory, blueprint registration, module-level `app`.
  Don't rename or move it.
- `src/{{ package_name }}/routes/` — **one module per HTTP resource**,
  each a Flask Blueprint. `items.py` is the example; a new resource is
  a new file here plus one `app.register_blueprint(...)` line in
  `main.py`.
{% if worker != "none" %}
- `src/{{ package_name }}/worker.py` — **the** {{ worker }} entrypoint:
  broker/app setup, importing every `tasks/` module so the worker
  process can discover them. Don't rename or move it.
- `src/{{ package_name }}/tasks/` — **one module per background job.**
  `example.py` is the demo; a new task is a new file here plus one
  import line in `worker.py` (see the comment there for why).
{% endif %}
- `src/{{ package_name }}/core/` — shared infrastructure every
  route/task might depend on: `config.py` (`Settings`, always), {% if database != "none" %}`db.py`
  (engine/session setup), {% endif %}{% if redis %}`redis.py` (the `redis_client`), {% endif %}and
  the natural home for anything similar later (e.g. a scheduler). Add
  new config fields to `Settings`, not scattered `os.environ` reads.
- `src/{{ package_name }}/schemas.py` — Pydantic request/response
  models, validated manually via `.model_validate()`/`.model_dump()`
  (Flask has no automatic request-body wiring the way FastAPI
  does).{% if database != "none" %} Not enforced the way `routes/`/`tasks/`/`core/` are — a `schemas/`
  folder is a reasonable next step once there's more than one resource.{% endif %}
{% if database != "none" %}
- `src/{{ package_name }}/models.py` — {{ orm }} models. Same story as
  `schemas.py`: fine as one file for one resource, free to become
  `models/` later.
{% endif %}
{% if migrations and orm == "flask-sqlalchemy" %}
- `migrations/` — Flask-Migrate migrations. New model → `uv run flask
  --app src/{{ package_name }}/main.py db migrate -m "..."`, review the
  generated file, then `uv run flask --app src/{{ package_name }}/main.py
  db upgrade`.
{% elif migrations and orm == "sqlalchemy" %}
- `alembic/` — migrations. New model → `uv run alembic revision
  --autogenerate -m "..."`, review the generated file, then `uv run
  alembic upgrade head`.
{% endif %}
- `tests/` — pytest tests{% if database != "none" %}, calling
  `create_app(database_url=..., testing=True)` directly with an
  isolated SQLite database — the production database configured in
  `.env` is never touched by the test suite{% endif %}.

## Conventions

- Dependencies are managed with `uv` via `pyproject.toml` — don't hand-edit
  `uv.lock`; run `uv add <package>` / `uv remove <package>` instead.
- Keep route handlers typed where practical; return `jsonify(...)`, not
  raw strings.
- New HTTP endpoints go in `routes/`, new background jobs go in
  `tasks/`, new shared infrastructure goes in `core/` — don't put any of
  these in `main.py`/`worker.py` themselves, which stay thin entrypoints.
{% if database != "none" and orm == "flask-sqlalchemy" %}
- New endpoints that touch the database use `db.session` (imported from
  `core.db`) — Flask-SQLAlchemy's scoped session, already bound to the
  request's app context. Don't create engines/sessions ad hoc.
{% elif database != "none" and orm == "sqlalchemy" %}
- New endpoints that touch the database call `get_session()` (imported
  from `core.db`) — a scoped session bound to the current app context.
  Don't create engines/sessions ad hoc.
{% endif %}
